// R20 Slice 2 — user attenuating functions via RELATIONAL refinements.
//
// A refinement return type whose predicate relates the result `_` to a function
// PARAMETER (not just a constant). This generalizes the kernel mint budget-carve
// obligation to any user-written attenuating function: "the value handed out is
// never more than what was available."
//
// When built with the `smt` feature a contract INSIDE the provable fragment is
// PROVEN for all inputs and the runtime check is elided; anything outside it is
// enforced at runtime instead — a violation exits 6.
//
// The two fns below are on opposite sides of that line, deliberately:
//   `carve`         — all params plain i64  → PROVEN statically
//   `clamp_request` — a param carries its own refinement (`cap: i64 where …`),
//                     which the v1 fragment does not admit → runtime-enforced
//
// Proving `clamp_request` needs the PRECONDITION `cap >= 0` encoded as an
// assumption (its postcondition `_ >= 0` holds only because of it); the v1
// encoder asserts no preconditions, so admitting the fn without them would
// yield a spurious counterexample at cap=-5. A warning is the honest answer
// until that lands. `axon verify` says which of the two happened per fn.
//
//   axon verify examples/refinement_attenuation.ax   # 1 proven, 1 left to the runtime gate
//   axon run    examples/refinement_attenuation.ax   # runs; checks elided/enforced accordingly

// Never carve out more than is available. `min_i64(g, avail) <= avail` holds for
// ALL integers, so this is unconditionally provable.
fn carve(grant: i64, avail: i64) -> (i64 where _ <= avail) {
    min_i64(grant, avail)
}

// Clamp a request into [0, cap]. The result is provably within both bounds —
// a two-sided relational refinement over the parameter `cap`.
fn clamp_request(req: i64, cap: i64 where _ >= 0) -> (i64 where _ >= 0 && _ <= cap) {
    min_i64(max_i64(req, 0), cap)
}

fn main() {
    println(to_str(carve(200, 50)))          // 50 — clamped to what's available
    println(to_str(carve(30, 50)))           // 30 — fits, handed out whole
    println(to_str(clamp_request(999, 100))) // 100
    println(to_str(clamp_request(0 - 5, 100)))   // 0
}
